90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Traffic signals are used at almost every busy road junction to control the movement of vehicles safely. Each road gets a turn to move \(green\), a short warning \(yellow\) and a waiting period \(red\). Simulating this behaviour is a good way to understand how data structures work together in a real-life problem.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (This project demonstrates the idea on a small scale. The user can add vehicles to any of the four roads using buttons on the web page. The browser \(frontend\) sends this request to the Java backend using a REST request. The backend validates the road name, adds the vehicle to the queue of that road, and on every second \(tick\) updates the signal and lets vehicles pass if their road has a green signal. The browser then shows the current state of the junction.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
